{"duration_api_ms":60178,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"77b1887a-3a93-47f0-a9f7-55ce9cfa0ca0","total_cost_usd":0.4068704,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":71862,"cache_read_input_tokens":76757,"output_tokens":5663,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1560},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":71862,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2072,"cache_read_input_tokens":72435,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3749,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3749},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":47343,"outputTokens":18,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.047433,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":5663,"cacheReadInputTokens":76757,"cacheCreationInputTokens":71862,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.3594374,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":1560,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":3,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"requirements\":[{\"id\":\"baseline-cluster\",\"requirement\":\"Three-member MariaDB Galera cluster running across node1, node2, node3 with application data\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"All three nodes independently report wsrep_cluster_size=3 and wsrep_local_state_comment=Synced after bootstrap/join. An appdata.orders table was created on node1 with 3 rows and confirmed replicated to node2 and node3 via direct SELECT, then a 4th row was inserted immediately before the outage.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-4ff238ca27eb4dd7\",\"cmd-0bafb7e9ae1642c6\",\"cmd-8b2c407a8dda4543\",\"cmd-594933ae1e6b4022\",\"cmd-d2f9e48afeed4368\",\"cmd-6cd049383f2647ec\",\"cmd-d91e79300df74e93\"]},{\"id\":\"full-shutdown\",\"requirement\":\"The cluster comes down completely for a maintenance window\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"mariadb was stopped in order (node2, node3, then node1 last, preserving safe_to_bootstrap:1/seqno 9 in node1's grastate.dat). Post-stop checks on all three nodes independently confirm systemctl is-active=inactive and no mysqld process, directly demonstrating a complete, bounded outage.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-3b25417a893b41a8\",\"cmd-b65b0cc7790349c5\",\"cmd-2859abb1d8d046c9\",\"cmd-fb64af3b5a4346a0\",\"cmd-8ca142eecfae449b\",\"cmd-e2dae255716f4140\"]},{\"id\":\"restore-with-data\",\"requirement\":\"Cluster brought back afterwards with every committed write still present and all three members serving again\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node1 was bootstrapped via galera_new_cluster and immediately showed all 4 rows including the pre-maintenance write. node2 and node3 were started and rejoined via SST; subsequent independent queries on each of the three nodes confirm wsrep_cluster_size=3, Synced, and identical 4-row data, directly showing the down-to-serving transition with no data loss.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-46a1cd19a0544108\",\"cmd-bb1ce802ac1241cc\",\"cmd-e6188fda5443480d\",\"cmd-c25bc0e1b1814544\",\"cmd-7d6b3d7947e94108\",\"cmd-76aa9e93a9454408\"]}],\"confidence\":0.93,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.85,\"summary\":\"Reviewed all state-changing commands chronologically. The hung 'yum -q check' processes holding the yum lock on all three nodes pre-existed the executor's session (started well before the executor's first action, consistent with before-prepare snapshots), so killing them (cmd-bf5588e906b94bf1, cmd-8d8aca7e6d894dfe, cmd-70fafd03af9e466c) was a necessary corrective prerequisite, as was the subsequent rpm --rebuilddb on node1 (cmd-1567cea34c854e8c) to repair the rpmdb corruption the killed process left behind. Repo additions, package installs, firewalld port openings, /etc/hosts edits, galera.cnf writes, mariadb enablement, and the ordered shutdown/restart/reboot sequence all map directly to satisfying the cluster-build, maintenance-window, and mandatory restart-evidence requirements — no unnecessary or unexplained mutations were found among them. The only residue is minor: /tmp/install.log, /tmp/install.pid, and /tmp/reboot.log were left behind on all three nodes per the after-executor vs. before-prepare temporary-files diff; these are low-risk log/pid artifacts with no security exposure, not database or credential residue, but they were not cleaned up.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-bf5588e906b94bf1\",\"cmd-8d8aca7e6d894dfe\",\"cmd-70fafd03af9e466c\",\"cmd-1567cea34c854e8c\",\"cmd-a78221d5e7e64640\",\"cmd-19fb998b29914901\",\"cmd-7c4a974b56a048f5\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node3:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All three material outcomes of the task — a running 3-node Galera cluster with application data, a complete cluster-wide outage for the maintenance window, and a full restoration with all committed writes present and all three members serving — are directly and coherently demonstrated by chronological, node-independent command evidence rather than by executor claims alone. The independent node-restart evidence protocol was also executed competently for all three nodes: pre-restart boot_id and cluster/data health, a reboot acknowledgement, an observably different post-restart boot_id (cross-checked against the fixed before-prepare and after-executor snapshots), and recovered cluster/data state on each node before proceeding to the next. Operational hygiene is good; the only blemish is a small amount of unremoved /tmp log/pid residue on each node from the install and reboot steps.\",\"limitations\":[\"Native package-manager consistency checks (package-health) timed out in both the before-prepare and after-executor snapshots on all three nodes, so package-database integrity after the rpmdb rebuild could not be independently cross-verified beyond the rpm -q checks the executor ran inline.\",\"Evidence for data replication during the SST/rejoin of node2 and node3 relies on post-join SELECT/COUNT queries rather than an observed in-flight resync log, though the before/after state transition itself is directly captured.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"baseline-cluster","requirement":"Three-member MariaDB Galera cluster running across node1, node2, node3 with application data","status":"satisfied","summary":"All three nodes independently report wsrep_cluster_size=3 and wsrep_local_state_comment=Synced after bootstrap/join. An appdata.orders table was created on node1 with 3 rows and confirmed replicated to node2 and node3 via direct SELECT, then a 4th row was inserted immediately before the outage.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4ff238ca27eb4dd7","cmd-0bafb7e9ae1642c6","cmd-8b2c407a8dda4543","cmd-594933ae1e6b4022","cmd-d2f9e48afeed4368","cmd-6cd049383f2647ec","cmd-d91e79300df74e93"]},{"id":"full-shutdown","requirement":"The cluster comes down completely for a maintenance window","status":"satisfied","summary":"mariadb was stopped in order (node2, node3, then node1 last, preserving safe_to_bootstrap:1/seqno 9 in node1's grastate.dat). Post-stop checks on all three nodes independently confirm systemctl is-active=inactive and no mysqld process, directly demonstrating a complete, bounded outage.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3b25417a893b41a8","cmd-b65b0cc7790349c5","cmd-2859abb1d8d046c9","cmd-fb64af3b5a4346a0","cmd-8ca142eecfae449b","cmd-e2dae255716f4140"]},{"id":"restore-with-data","requirement":"Cluster brought back afterwards with every committed write still present and all three members serving again","status":"satisfied","summary":"node1 was bootstrapped via galera_new_cluster and immediately showed all 4 rows including the pre-maintenance write. node2 and node3 were started and rejoined via SST; subsequent independent queries on each of the three nodes confirm wsrep_cluster_size=3, Synced, and identical 4-row data, directly showing the down-to-serving transition with no data loss.","evidence_ids":["cmd-46a1cd19a0544108","cmd-bb1ce802ac1241cc","cmd-e6188fda5443480d","cmd-c25bc0e1b1814544","cmd-7d6b3d7947e94108","cmd-76aa9e93a9454408"]}],"confidence":0.93,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.85,"summary":"Reviewed all state-changing commands chronologically. The hung 'yum -q check' processes holding the yum lock on all three nodes pre-existed the executor's session (started well before the executor's first action, consistent with before-prepare snapshots), so killing them (cmd-bf5588e906b94bf1, cmd-8d8aca7e6d894dfe, cmd-70fafd03af9e466c) was a necessary corrective prerequisite, as was the subsequent rpm --rebuilddb on node1 (cmd-1567cea34c854e8c) to repair the rpmdb corruption the killed process left behind. Repo additions, package installs, firewalld port openings, /etc/hosts edits, galera.cnf writes, mariadb enablement, and the ordered shutdown/restart/reboot sequence all map directly to satisfying the cluster-build, maintenance-window, and mandatory restart-evidence requirements — no unnecessary or unexplained mutations were found among them. The only residue is minor: /tmp/install.log, /tmp/install.pid, and /tmp/reboot.log were left behind on all three nodes per the after-executor vs. before-prepare temporary-files diff; these are low-risk log/pid artifacts with no security exposure, not database or credential residue, but they were not cleaned up.","evidence_ids":["cmd-bf5588e906b94bf1","cmd-8d8aca7e6d894dfe","cmd-70fafd03af9e466c","cmd-1567cea34c854e8c","cmd-a78221d5e7e64640","cmd-19fb998b29914901","cmd-7c4a974b56a048f5","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services"]},"overall_summary":"All three material outcomes of the task — a running 3-node Galera cluster with application data, a complete cluster-wide outage for the maintenance window, and a full restoration with all committed writes present and all three members serving — are directly and coherently demonstrated by chronological, node-independent command evidence rather than by executor claims alone. The independent node-restart evidence protocol was also executed competently for all three nodes: pre-restart boot_id and cluster/data health, a reboot acknowledgement, an observably different post-restart boot_id (cross-checked against the fixed before-prepare and after-executor snapshots), and recovered cluster/data state on each node before proceeding to the next. Operational hygiene is good; the only blemish is a small amount of unremoved /tmp log/pid residue on each node from the install and reboot steps.","limitations":["Native package-manager consistency checks (package-health) timed out in both the before-prepare and after-executor snapshots on all three nodes, so package-database integrity after the rpmdb rebuild could not be independently cross-verified beyond the rpm -q checks the executor ran inline.","Evidence for data replication during the SST/rejoin of node2 and node3 relies on post-join SELECT/COUNT queries rather than an observed in-flight resync log, though the before/after state transition itself is directly captured."]},"ttft_ms":21061,"type":"result","duration_ms":58659,"uuid":"ebb48bf1-f252-4a76-876c-7bf42382ff3f","ttft_stream_ms":1312,"time_to_request_ms":40,"first_content_frame_ms":1701,"queued_turn_count":0}
